# --- secret shapes (added 2026-08-24 after R455) ---------------------------------
# A live USPTO API key reached a PUBLIC repo because .uspto_key was documented as
# "gitignored" and never actually added to any .gitignore. .env* was covered here;
# nothing else was. These patterns are about SHAPE, not about the one file that
# leaked: the next secret will have a name nobody predicted.
*.key
*.pem
*.p12
*.pfx
*.keystore
*_key
.*_key
credentials*
*secret*
*.token
token.txt

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PORTED 2026-09-07. These rules existed ONLY on feat/partner-toolkit-m0 - a branch
# that must never be merged (it reverts main) - so the protection they describe has
# never been on main, on any PR branch, or in any fresh clone. Nothing is exposed
# today: git ls-tree origin/main shows 0 files under .claude/ and 0 under emails/.
# But this repository is PUBLIC, and the guard was one branch switch from vanishing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# keep-working Stop-hook sentinel + counter (machine-local, never committed)
.claude/KEEP_WORKING
.claude/.keep_working_count
.claude/STOP_REASON

# Correspondence with users. THIS REPO IS PUBLIC: these files carry real names and
# email addresses, and were untracked-but-unignored, i.e. one `git add -A` away from
# being published (the R135 trap, previously hit with MISTAKES.md).
emails/

# local D1 scan-budget counter (volatile, machine-local)
.claude/hooks/.d1_budget.json

# reliability-system session state (machine-local receipts)
.claude/state/

# Internal operational docs - never in the public tree (2026-08-31, see R531)
.claude/

# ...AND THE REST OF THE SAME CLASS, swept 2026-09-07 instead of fixing the one instance
# that was reported. Porting `emails/` and `.claude/` off the unmergeable branch closed two
# rules; it never asked what ELSE was untracked-but-unignored in a PUBLIC repo. Measured with
# `git check-ignore -v`: `.session-catchup/` was already covered, these three were not, and
# `git status` listed all three as `??` - one `git add -A` from publication.
#
# NUMBERS.md is the operational numbers ledger: costs, D1 detail, `D:/temp/claude/...` paths.
# It is a sibling of .claude/MISTAKES.md in everything but its location.
NUMBERS.md
# run logs, ANYWHERE. This started as `pipeline/_*.log`, which was the shape I happened to be
# looking at and could not reach `clients/python/examples/run_momentum.log`. Measured before
# widening: `git ls-files | grep -c '\.log$'` is 0, so this is additive.
*.log
# a file literally named `nul` is always an accident: a Windows `> nul` redirect run under a
# POSIX shell creates one instead of discarding output.
nul
#
# SESSION HANDOFF TRANSCRIPTS. THE FIRST SWEEP CLAIMED THIS WAS ALREADY COVERED AND IT WAS NOT:
# `git check-ignore -v .session-catchup/` printed `.gitignore:31:` with an EMPTY pattern - line 31
# is BLANK - and I read that as a match. The instruments that answer honestly are
# `git status --porcelain` (`?? .session-catchup/`) and `git ls-files -o --exclude-standard`,
# which counted **20 untracked, unexcluded files** under it in the main checkout. They are decoded
# chat transcripts carrying real e-mail addresses: the `emails/` hazard exactly, one `git add -A`
# from publication in a PUBLIC repo.
.session-catchup/
# Derived data, 1.4 GB and 13 MB. `data/` tracks exactly four files (tickers, metadata,
# ticker_meta, classify_tickers.py) and none is under either of these.
data/clean_full/
data/playground/
#
# NOT ignored, deliberately: `docs/` holds documents meant for this repo
# (VENDOR_SEAM_BASIS_20260904.md, UNAPPLIED_SPLITS_20260905.md), and the untracked
# `pipeline/*.py` and `assets/*.svg` are content decisions, not hazards.
